---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';

const phases = [
  {
    name: 'Assess',
    body: 'A project review — the short structured conversation — and then, where warranted, the paid audit: budget, contract, schedule and team examined before anything is binding.',
    deliverable: 'Written findings on budget, contract, schedule and team, plus a risk register.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Commit',
    body: 'The period between choosing a builder and signing with one. Bids compared line against line, the contract reviewed and negotiated with your counsel, and responsibility for every scope item mapped to a party before it can fall between two.',
    deliverable: 'Redlines, bid analysis, scope matrix.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Hold',
    body: 'Construction, run as a discipline. We chair the project meetings, own the decision log, and maintain a live schedule against the real baseline — not the one that drifts a week at a time until nobody remembers the original.',
    deliverable: 'Minutes, decision log, current baseline.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Verify',
    body: 'Every payment application read against work in place before it is approved. Every change order interrogated: was it in scope, is the price supported, what does it do to the schedule. Draws approved in writing, or not.',
    deliverable: 'Monthly owner’s report and signed draw approvals.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Close',
    body: 'The stage exhaustion usually surrenders: punch list driven to closure, warranties and as-builts collected, lien releases confirmed, and the final reconciliation done while leverage still exists.',
    deliverable: 'Closeout package.',
  },
];
---

<Article
  title="How an Engagement Runs — Five Phases"
  heading="How an engagement runs"
  kicker="Method"
  description="Five phases — assess, commit, hold, verify, close — each with named deliverables. Concrete artifacts, not assurances, from due diligence through final reconciliation."
>
  <p>
    Five phases, each producing named artifacts — documents you receive, not assurances you are given. An
    engagement can begin at any phase, but the earlier ones are where the leverage lives: everything after
    signatures is played from the position the paperwork created.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-10">
    {
      phases.map((phase, i) => (
        <section class="border-t border-rule py-8" aria-label={phase.name}>
          <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-[12rem_1fr]">
            <div>
              <p class="annotation">Phase {i + 1}</p>
              <h2 class="mt-1 text-xl font-semibold">{phase.name}</h2>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="max-w-prose text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">{phase.body}</p>
              <p class="annotation mt-4">Deliverable: {phase.deliverable}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>If you aren’t local</h2>
  <p>
    Remote clients get the same engagement with the documentation discipline turned up. Reporting runs on a
    fixed cadence you can plan around, in writing, with photographs selected to inform rather than reassure.
    Site verification is performed by retained licensed inspectors in the project’s region and reviewed
    centrally. Draw approvals are transmitted in writing, recorded against each payment application, and
    archived — so the project file reads the same whether you visited weekly or never.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ready to see where your project sits? <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
